---
/* What Fanout does not do, on the front page rather than buried.
 *
 * A landing page that only lists capabilities makes a reader work out the
 * limits from silence, and these are the limits that decide whether Fanout fits
 * at all. Someone who needs multi-tenancy should learn it in thirty seconds
 * here, not in an hour from a failed evaluation.
 *
 * The content is a Markdown list passed as children, not data in this file.
 * That is the same rule the section headings follow — copy held in a component
 * renders for a person and vanishes from `<path>.md` and llms.txt, and here it
 * would have meant the page's limits being the one thing an agent could not
 * read. An earlier version of this component held the list itself and the page
 * repeated it in Markdown alongside, which rendered every refusal twice.
 *
 * Each item is `**claim** — detail`, so the styling below leans on <strong> to
 * find the claim rather than asking the author for a second structure.
 */
---

<div class="fo-refusals">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .fo-refusals {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .fo-refusals :global(ul) {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .fo-refusals :global(ul) {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
    }
  }

  .fo-refusals :global(li) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5);
    border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: var(--fo-font-body);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
  }

  /* The claim is the mono half; the explanation after the dash stays in the
     body face, so a reader scanning only the bold text still gets the list. */
  .fo-refusals :global(li strong) {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    font-family: var(--fo-font-display);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
  }
</style>
